    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Geologic structures such as folds, are not comprised of single flexed layers but of inhomogeneous stacks of rock strata. By including the inhomogeneous character of geologic materials and the potential for inelastic deformation at bedding interfaces into a bending model, the deformation of rock strata is more closely approximated. The development of fracture networks within geologic structures can be determined from the three-dimensional stress state and the subsequent flow of fluids throughout the structures better understood.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
